From fdafc0a4a2a6c113ab96e34150281699ba170807 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 08:22:27 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Merkle: static forwarders for ancestors nested in or reached
 through an object

The `mirror` rule recompiles a top-level object whose ancestor changed,
for the static forwarders its mirror (or companion) class has for every
member it inherits. It looked the changed class up among the object's
stored parents by name, and the two names differ for an upstream class
nested in an object: the change names it by its binary name, `p.Q$A`,
the stored linearization by its source name, `p.Q.A` (`p.Q$.A` under
Scala 3). A parent reached through another object, `object O extends
Inst.A` with A a member class of `class Outer`, is stored as `p.Inst.A`,
which no class is called. In either case the object did not recompile,
and a Java client calling the forwarder failed with NoSuchMethodError
once the member's signature changed.

Compare names in canonical form, and when the object has no companion
class, so that every inheritance edge of its name is the object's, also
take its ancestors from HierarchyView, which follows the external
inheritance relation.

merkle-x-mirror-nested and merkle-x-mirror-instance are merkle-x-mirror
with the ancestor nested in an object and reached through one; both fail
without this change. merkle-mirror-companion-added adds a member to the
ancestor of an object with a companion class, which holds the
forwarders; it passes either way.
